# Local environment. Copy to `.env` to override anything below:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# Copying is optional. `scripts/with-env.ts` — which every `pnpm` script for the
# local stack goes through — loads this file as the baseline, then `.env` on top
# of it, then anything already exported in the shell, which wins. So a fresh
# clone runs `pnpm dev:stack` with no setup step, and `.env` only ever holds the
# lines a developer actually changed.
#
# Format: one KEY=value per line. No inline comments, no shell expansion.
# `.env` is gitignored; this file is the documentation and must stay safe to
# commit — local defaults only, never a real credential.

# --- Local Postgres container (docker-compose.yml) --------------------------
# These configure the container itself. They are not application configuration:
# the application only ever reads DATABASE_URL.
POSTGRES_USER=game_shop
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=game_shop
POSTGRES_DB=game_shop
# Host port. 5433 rather than 5432, which is routinely taken by another local
# Postgres. Change this and DATABASE_URL together.
POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# --- Application (technical-considerations §2.7) -----------------------------

# Postgres connection string. Locally this points at the Compose container; on
# Vercel it is Neon's *pooled* endpoint, since a function instance holds a pool
# of one and fifty concurrent invocations must not exhaust the connection limit.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://game_shop:game_shop@localhost:5433/game_shop

# Port the NestJS API listens on.
API_PORT=3000

# API base the frontend calls. In development this is where the Vite dev server
# proxies /api and /internal (see apps/web/vite.config.ts) — the browser itself
# always uses same-origin relative paths, which is also what the deployed system
# serves. Not consumed by browser code; it never reaches the bundle.
WEB_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Where the payment simulator delivers the events it mints — the address a real
# payment provider would be given for our webhook. `POST /api/payments/:orderId/
# simulate` builds a contract-shaped event and POSTs it here over HTTP rather
# than calling the receiver in-process, for the same reason SUPPLIER_A_URL below
# is a real URL: the boundary is the thing being demonstrated. Locally that is a
# loopback call to this same process, and it is still a real request — it is
# serialised, parsed and answered with a status code. In deployment it names the
# API's own public origin, where the two sides are genuinely two function
# invocations. The full endpoint, not a base: there is exactly one address a
# payment event is ever delivered to.
#
# The API refuses to start without it, or if it is not an http: or https: URL:
# the simulator is the only way anything gets paid, so an API that came up not
# knowing where to deliver would look healthy and be unable to take money. Read
# through the same validators as SUPPLIER_A_URL below (apps/api/src/config/env.ts).
PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_URL=http://localhost:3000/api/webhooks/payment

# Base URL of the supplier A stub — the supplier asked first. A real URL even
# locally: issuance crosses an HTTP boundary the shop is meant to distrust. The
# issuance client appends the path, calling POST {SUPPLIER_A_URL}/issue. A
# trailing slash is fine — it is stripped when the value is read.
#
# The API refuses to start without it, and refuses to start if it is not an
# http: or https: URL: a shop that boots not knowing where its supplier lives
# looks healthy right up until the first paid order, which is the worst possible
# moment to discover a typo. Validated in apps/api/src/config/supplier-config.ts
# and injected as SUPPLIER_A_CONFIG.
#
# THE TRAP THE http:// GUARD EXISTS FOR, since it is not obvious:
#
#     SUPPLIER_A_URL=localhost:3000/internal/suppliers/a
#
# PARSES. `new URL()` accepts it, reading "localhost:" as the scheme and leaving
# the hostname empty. Nothing complains until fetch is handed it, on a paid
# order. Checking the protocol is what catches it; parsing is not.
SUPPLIER_A_URL=http://localhost:3000/internal/suppliers/a

# Base URL of the supplier B stub — the BACKUP, reached only after supplier A
# answers with a definite refusal (never while an attempt is still unknown; see
# architecture.md §4). Everything said about SUPPLIER_A_URL above applies here
# word for word, including the missing-scheme trap:
#
#     SUPPLIER_B_URL=localhost:3000/internal/suppliers/b
#
# PARSES CLEANLY, as scheme "localhost:" with an empty hostname, and fails only
# inside fetch — at the first fall-through, on an order somebody has already
# paid for, where it looks exactly like supplier B being down. Validated by the
# same readUrl (apps/api/src/config/env.ts), injected as SUPPLIER_B_CONFIG, and
# never read with a `??` default: a default would make a MISSING variable
# indistinguishable from a correct one on a laptop and silently wrong elsewhere.
#
# The API refuses to start without it, for a consequence one step further out
# than A's: with no backup configured the shop still sells, right up until the
# first refusal from A, at which point there is nowhere to fall through to and a
# recoverable refusal becomes a failed delivery.
#
# Both stubs are hosted in this same process locally and draw from ONE fifty-key
# pool and ONE ledger (technical-considerations §3, A4). One consequence worth
# knowing before it looks like a bug: an empty pool is refused by A and by B, so
# an out-of-stock order costs one wasted fall-through call and leaves TWO
# attempt rows rather than one.
SUPPLIER_B_URL=http://localhost:3000/internal/suppliers/b

# Client-side timeout on supplier calls, in milliseconds. One value for BOTH
# suppliers, deliberately: the deadline is a property of what the shop can
# afford to wait, it is one term in the ordered chain below, and the invocation
# budget multiplies it by the number of providers. Present from Phase 1 so
# Phase 3 changes only its value.
#
# THE ORDERED CHAIN (architecture.md §5). One inequality always holds, because
# it is what makes a timeout observable at all:
#
#     SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS  <  function execution ceiling
#                             (the platform's, not ours)
#
# When our own client gives up first, the shop learns "no answer" as a value and
# records the attempt as unknown, keeps the request_id and retries the same
# supplier. When the platform's ceiling arrives first the function is killed
# mid-flight — no exception, no log line, no attempt row updated — and the order
# is left in delivering with a key that may or may not have been issued. So: a
# timeout must always be observed as a timeout, never as a killed function.
#
# WHERE THE SUPPLIER'S INJECTED HANG GOES IS NOT ONE FIXED ANSWER. hang_ms is a
# column on the supplier's own supplier_behaviour row, armed per check, and the
# two scenarios worth staging need OPPOSITE orderings:
#
#   Slow but successful — a short hang placed BEFORE the key claim:
#
#       hang_ms  <  SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS  <  ceiling
#
#   No timeout occurs and none is meant to; the call completes normally. The
#   point is that a slow supplier is not a failed one.
#
#   The timeout trap — a long hang placed AFTER the key claim commits:
#
#       SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS  <  hang_ms  <  ceiling
#
#   A key is genuinely issued, our client times out and cannot know it, and the
#   re-probe on the same request_id gets the same code back from the supplier's
#   ledger. The PLACEMENT (after the claim) is what puts a code on file; the
#   DURATION (past our deadline) is what makes us time out at all. Both, or the
#   scenario does not happen.
#
# THE PLACEMENT IS A COLUMN, NOT A CONSTANT: `hang_before_claim` on the
# supplier's own supplier_behaviour row, set through
# PUT /internal/suppliers/:provider/behaviour beside hang_ms. It defaults to
# false — AFTER the claim, the trap — because that is the scenario this phase
# exists to demonstrate; `{"hang_before_claim": true}` asks for the other one.
# Neither the placement nor hang_ms is an environment variable: both belong to
# the simulated supplier and are armed per check, while SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS
# below belongs to the shop.
#
# THIS WAS DOCUMENTED BACKWARDS UNTIL PHASE 3, as "hang < SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS <
# ceiling" for every case. Read literally it produces no timeout: the client
# waits, the supplier answers, and the check goes green having exercised
# nothing. The old text is not simply inverted — it describes the first scenario
# while being cited as the basis for the second.
#
# The reasoning given for it conflated two different events, and they are worth
# keeping apart:
#
#   - OUR CLIENT GIVING UP. AbortSignal.timeout aborts OUR SOCKET
#     (apps/api/src/issuance/supplier.client.ts). It does NOT stop the
#     supplier's handler, which keeps running, can claim a key, and can finish
#     and write a response nobody is listening for. That is exactly what makes a
#     timeout "unknown" rather than "failed". Whether the claim happens is
#     decided by where the hang sits relative to it, not by how our deadline
#     compares to it.
#   - THE PLATFORM KILLING THE FUNCTION. That is the ceiling's doing, not the
#     timeout's, and it is the collapse the first inequality above prevents.
#
# Decimal digits only, greater than zero. "2e3", "0x7d0", "1.5" and "2000ms" are
# rejected at startup rather than coerced, because a timeout that is secretly a
# different number than the one written here makes the chain above impossible to
# verify by reading it.
SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS=2000

# How many times ONE request id may be asked before the shop stops asking and
# records that what happened to it was never established. Every ask in this
# budget sends the SAME three arguments to deriveIssuanceRequestId and therefore
# the same id, so each one is the same question — "did my earlier request produce
# a key?" — answered from the supplier's own ledger. It is not a retry count with
# a different name: retrying means asking a DIFFERENT supplier, which is the
# ladder's fall-through, and this number bounds the rung that exists to make
# fall-through unnecessary.
#
# THE ONLY SUPPLIER VARIABLE IN THIS FILE WHOSE ABSENCE IS NOT FATAL, and the
# only one with no live line of its own. Unset means 3 — one ask and two
# re-probes, spec 003's assumption A1 — so an `.env` written before this variable
# existed still boots unchanged. That 3 lives in exactly one place,
# DEFAULT_SUPPLIER_MAX_PROBES_PER_REQUEST in apps/api/src/config/supplier-config.ts;
# a live line here would be a second copy of it to disagree with, and the boot
# line below prints whichever value is actually in force anyway. Set it in `.env`,
# or inline for one run:
#
#     SUPPLIER_MAX_PROBES_PER_REQUEST=2
#
# Absence is the only thing that relaxes. A value that IS present goes through
# the same validator as SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS above, so "2e3", "0" and "three" stop
# the boot rather than being coerced into something nobody wrote.
#
# THE INVOCATION BUDGET — the term phase 3 added to the ordered chain above, and
# the one inequality nothing in this process can enforce (spec 003 R5).
#
# The ladder now walks to a resting state inside ONE invocation, so the worst
# case a single request can spend on suppliers is the product of three factors:
#
#     SUPPLIER_MAX_PROBES_PER_REQUEST × SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS × |supplierLadder|
#         +  overhead   <   function execution ceiling
#
# With the defaults that is 3 × 2000 × 2 = 12 000 ms, and 12 S EXCEEDS VERCEL'S
# HOBBY CEILING. Nothing here refuses a boot over that, and nothing should try:
# the ceiling is the platform's, it is not in the environment, and a number
# hardcoded to compare against would be a guess that one day fails a boot over a
# limit that no longer applies. The constraint can only be made VISIBLE, never
# enforced — which is what the boot line is for. IssuanceRunnerService's
# constructor (apps/api/src/issuance/issuance-runner.service.ts) logs the product
# with every factor beside it, in the same startup output as the two supplier
# lines:
#
#     issuance budget: worst-case supplier time for one ladder walk, excluding overhead
#     worst_case_ms: 12000, max_probes_per_request: 3, timeout_ms: 2000, providers: 2
#
# TWO DIALS FOR A DEPLOYED PROFILE, and only two, because the third factor is the
# ladder's length and that is retry policy rather than configuration: lower
# SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS, or lower SUPPLIER_MAX_PROBES_PER_REQUEST to 2. Change one,
# boot, read the line again. That re-read is the check; there is no other.
#
# WHEN THE CEILING WINS ANYWAY — the part that makes this survivable rather than
# alarming. The function is killed mid-walk: no exception, no `catch`, no log
# line, nothing to grep for. What survives is the row, because
# issuance_attempts.status is written `unknown` BEFORE the supplier call rather
# than by a handler after it, so no error path has to run for it to stay
# truthful. The order is left in `delivering` with its newest attempt `unknown`,
# its payment event still pending (processed_at NULL, so the other processing
# triggers can still find it), and the operator recovery list surfaces exactly
# that shape. Recoverable and listed, not lost — and a retry re-asks the SAME
# request_id, which the supplier's ledger answers with the code it already issued
# if it issued one.
#
# THE COUPLING TO SHUTDOWN_DRAIN_TIMEOUT_MS (a constant, not a variable:
# apps/api/src/scheduling/tracked-continuation-scheduler.ts), which moved with
# this budget and now sits deliberately BELOW a full walk. That bound used to sit
# above the longest legitimate continuation and below the shortest grace period
# before SIGKILL; a 12 s walk makes those two mutually exclusive, since the
# tightest supervisor that really exists — apps/api/test/concurrency/support/
# api-instance.ts, used by the concurrency suite and by scripts/race/run-checks.ts
# — SIGKILLs 5 000 ms after SIGTERM. (Not `docker stop`: docker-compose.yml runs
# only Postgres, so it never signals this process at all.) The first constraint
# is the one that gives, at 3 000 ms, for the same reason the ceiling above is
# survivable: abandoning a walk mid-flight is recoverable and LOUD — the give-up
# line names the order_id and the event_id — while overrunning the grace period
# is SILENT, a SIGKILL mid-drain with no line at all. 4 000 was measured and
# rejected (the process exited 4 790 ms after SIGTERM, 210 ms inside a 5 000 ms
# grace); at 3 000 the headroom is 1 945 ms. Raising SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS does not
# require raising that bound — the two are coupled through this budget, not
# through each other.

# Shared bearer token for the admin surface. A single shared token is the
# assignment's stated minimum for authentication (architecture.md §6). Two
# routes are behind it today:
#
#   POST /api/admin/payment-events/sweep     the fourth of architecture.md §4's
#                                            processing triggers, and the
#                                            backstop for whatever the other
#                                            three missed
#   GET  /api/admin/orders/undelivered       every order that was paid for and
#                                            is holding no key (spec 003 §2.4)
#
# Send it as `Authorization: Bearer <value>`. A missing or wrong token is 401.
#
# UNLIKE EVERY OTHER VARIABLE ABOVE, LEAVING THIS UNSET DOES NOT STOP THE BOOT.
# The API logs one line at `error` naming the consequence and comes up with the
# admin surface DISABLED — it answers 503, never 200, so an unset token can only
# ever close the door and never open it. That asymmetry is deliberate: the four
# processing triggers are layered so no single one is load-bearing, so a missing
# admin token costs a backstop rather than an order, and refusing to boot over
# it would take the catalogue, order creation and the webhook down with it.
#
# WHAT IT NOW ALSO COSTS, and it is worth stating because it is newer than the
# paragraph above: with this unset there is no way to see an order that was paid
# for and never delivered. The shop keeps selling and keeps delivering; the one
# screen that shows what it failed to deliver answers 503 to everybody.
#
# What IS refused at startup is a token shorter than 16 characters. Absence is
# the state of a fresh clone and of the middle of a rotation; a two-character
# token is neither — it is a lock somebody asked for that opens to a guess, and
# unlike absence it does not fail closed.
#
# Generate a real one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
# The value below is a local default and is not a secret. Anything reachable
# from outside a laptop must set its own.
ADMIN_TOKEN=local-dev-admin-token-not-a-secret

# --- Client-supplied order id (test affordance) ------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE, AND NOTHING BELOW IS A LIVE LINE — same treatment
# as RACE_BASE_URLS further down, and for a related reason: setting this in a
# committed file would change what POST /api/orders accepts for everyone who
# runs the local stack, not just for the one script that needs it.
#
# ALLOW_CLIENT_SUPPLIED_ORDER_ID lets POST /api/orders accept an explicit
# `id` in its JSON body instead of minting one (architecture.md §9, "A test
# affordance on order creation"). It exists for exactly two callers: the
# seeds, and scripts/race/before-order.ts — the "webhook before order" check,
# which has to name an order id before the order exists so it can deliver a
# `paid` webhook for that id first and create the order with it second, on a
# different API instance. Nothing else in this codebase can pick an order id
# in advance; every other caller gets a server-minted `ord_` + ULID.
#
# Off by default, and fails CLOSED: with the variable unset, an `id` field in
# the body is rejected with 400 rather than silently ignored — a caller that
# thinks its chosen id was used and gets a random one instead is a confusing,
# silent failure for the one caller this field exists to serve. A duplicate
# `id` while the flag IS set is a clean 409, never a 500.
#
# `pnpm race`'s local harness sets this automatically for the instances it
# spawns, so nobody runs `pnpm race` needs to set it by hand.
#
# The API refuses to start if this is set to anything other than exactly
# "true" or "false" — matching this file's stance elsewhere (SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS
# above) that a value which would silently coerce to something nobody wrote is
# refused at boot rather than guessed at.
#
# MUST NEVER BE SET IN AN ENVIRONMENT REACHABLE BY REAL SHOPPERS. Enabling it
# lets any caller of POST /api/orders choose another order's primary key.
#
#     ALLOW_CLIENT_SUPPLIED_ORDER_ID=true

# --- Dev server -------------------------------------------------------------
# Port the Vite dev server listens on.
WEB_PORT=5173

# --- The adversarial race checks (`pnpm race`) ------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT SET HERE, AND NOTHING BELOW IS A LIVE LINE.
#
# RACE_BASE_URLS is the comma-separated list of API origins the checks in
# scripts/race/ round-robin their requests across. `pnpm race` starts four real
# apps/api processes on ports 4201-4204, sets this for the checks, and stops
# them again — so the value is produced by the run, not configured for it.
#
# Setting it in this file (or in .env) would be actively harmful: with-env.ts
# exports it on every run, so `pnpm race` would stop spawning local instances
# and silently start expecting somebody else's. Set it inline instead, which is
# how the deployed target is checked — one origin is correct there, because
# Vercel supplies the separate instances:
#
#     RACE_BASE_URLS=https://game-shop.vercel.app pnpm race
#
# Why a list at all: packages/db pins the pool to max: 1 per process, so fifty
# concurrent requests to ONE local process serialise in Node before Postgres
# sees them and a shop with no locking at all passes every check (measured —
# architecture.md §7). The list is what puts the checks in front of genuinely
# separate OS processes.
#
# The other knobs, with their defaults, all optional:
#
#     RACE_INSTANCES=4              local instances to start
#     RACE_BASE_PORT=4201           first port; clear of 3000/5173 and 4101-4104
#     RACE_SKIP_BUILD=              non-empty skips the rebuild — WRONG for RED
#                                   validation, since instances run dist/
#     RACE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS=180000  per check
#     RACE_VERBOSE=                 non-empty also streams instance stdout
#
# See scripts/race/README.md.
